I mean, that kind of idea of people turning in. up in, you know, to have huge kind of festivals, large crowds. I mean, obviously the, the vibe is very different. The vibe is different. But it's so often, things that seem distinctive about the 60s are being prefigured in kind of unsettling ways. I guess so. Yeah. I think what it takes inspiration from a lot is sort of state fairs. Have you ever been to one of these state fairs? No. I went to the Minnesota state fair once. Is it fun? I probably wouldn't go again deep down. I mean, mainly a lot of it seems to be eating these horrendous snacks, kind of corn dogs or whatever. But the idea of turning up in an enormous field and having a great festival.

13
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:25.000
Yeah, Americans love that. That's a thing, isn't it? Yeah, totally it is. But this, I mean, this did not happen at Woodstock. The Kokomo picnic, like most of these picnics, they will end with a cross burning on a hilltop. And there would be searchlights and flare. Sometimes there'd be fireworks. And these are great spectacles.

14
00:07:25.000 --> 00:08:01.000
I mean, they're very expensive. But they pay for themselves because they're... great for recruitment. So there will be hundreds, if not thousands of people who will join the clan en masse on these occasions. And your point about, you know, woodstock and festivals and whatnot, this is the side of the clan that I think would surprise listeners who think of, well, the clan is obviously a hateful paramilitary organisation. This is the clan as family fun, as a fun day out. So actually, if you live in Oregon or you live in Indiana or somewhere, and you have kids and you're white and you're Protestant.

15
00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:33.000
Going to one of these clan picnics, you know, it's a fun way to spend the day. And it's part of this huge social calendar. So the historian, social historian, Kathleen Blee, says, with the myriad of weddings, baby christenings, family picnics, athletic contests, parades, spelling bees, beauty contests, rodeos and circuses, the clan basically became, and I quote, an ordinary normal, taken for granted part of the life of the white Protestant majority. And when historians later did interviews with clan members, the thing that the clan members

16
00:08:33.000 --> 00:09:08.000
from the 20s and were to about millions of people often said was, it was just, it was good fun. It was a laugh. We went along and people dressed up and there were circus stunts and there was sizzling burgers. I mean, this is wholesome fun rather than the castrating people and throwing them into the lake. Well, there's always that element, of course, to life in the clan. I don't want to undersell that element at all. But I think it is important to emphasise that it's not just about vigilante violence. So another aspect of this, which will surprise people, is how many women are involved.

17
00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:25.000
